MAC hook arguments and skipped callbacks
========================================

The existing mac_framework:::hook-start and hook-done contracts are unchanged.
A called policy produces start, optional args, callback execution, then done.
Start/args/done nesting can be correlated by thread and a script-maintained
stack. Enabling different events mid-call may produce unmatched events.

mac_framework:::hook-args:
  arg0: kernel string naming the hook (for example vnode_check_open).
  arg1: kernel string naming the policy.
  arg2: number of callback arguments, excluding the policy dispatch context.
  arg3: const uint64_t * containing argument slots in declaration order.

mac-hook-arguments.json provides names, types and encodings for every callback
slot in mac_policy_ops. It is generated from mac_policy.h together with
sys/security/mac/mac_trace_args.h. Run mac-hook-arguments.py after ABI edits;
--check detects stale output. Zero-argument hooks emit argc=0; do not read
slots in that case. Current callbacks have at most eight arguments.

Each scalar is converted to its declared callback type and then uint64_t;
signed values can be recovered by casting to their documented type. Pointers
are converted through uintptr_t. A by-value struct (currently the two timespec
arguments of vnode_check_setutimes) is encoded as the address of a captured
copy. That copy and the argument array exist only during this synchronous
probe. Never retain these array/struct addresses for later dereference.
Pointers to callback objects have their ordinary kernel lifetimes; copy the
required fields at hook-args instead of assuming they survive hook-done.
The probe does not copy strings, label contents, credentials or other objects.

Argument expressions run exactly once per invoked callback, even with multiple
probes enabled. Snapshot array construction and scalar conversions reside in
the SDT patchpoint's enabled branch: a disabled hook-args probe does not build
the array. Snapshot capture does not execute callbacks skipped by composition.
The extra patchpoints still have a small disabled instrumentation footprint;
this is not a claim of zero performance cost.

mac_framework:::hook-skip:
  arg0: hook name; arg1: policy name; arg2: reason.
  1: this traversed policy has no implementation of the hook.
  2: a present boolean hook was skipped by && or || short-circuiting.

A skip is not a denial and has no matching start/done or argument snapshot.
No-policy fast paths and checks that never enter framework dispatch do not
produce per-policy skips. Policy-specific syscall lookup reports a skip only
for a matching policy name with no syscall callback. It does not enumerate
unrelated policy names. Existing MAC result and policy-decision probes still
carry their original meanings.

BSDInstruments profiles:
  mac-hooks: per-policy/hook latency and raw results, plus skip reason counts.
  mac-hook-arguments: prints all argument slots without dereferencing objects.

Example: to inspect vnode_check_open credentials and vnode, filter hook-args
using stringof(arg0) == "vnode_check_open". Interpret slot 0 as struct ucred *,
slot 1 as struct vnode *, slot 2 as struct label *, slot 3 as accmode_t.
For file_check_ioctl the slots are cred, file, fd and command respectively.
Use the generated contract rather than assuming identical argument positions
across different hooks.

Validation exercises real dispatch macros with synthetic policies: absent
callbacks, short circuits, order/locking, one-time argument evaluation, scalar
width/sign, pointer slots, zero/eight arguments, by-value timespec, and disabled
argument capture. Kernel compile checks include all MAC framework C files
with/without KDTRACE_HOOKS. Live probe firing still requires a DTrace-enabled
kernel and device; the current host does not expose that device.
